];
---
<QuestionLayout
  title="How do I verify a Traverse model package signature before I run it?"
  description="Run traverse-cli model verify with the public key you trust. v0.17.1 ships an empty production key list. The test-only digits-mlp key is not a production trust root."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-do-i-verify-a-traverse-model-package-signature.html"
  category="Runtime and architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> run <code>traverse-cli model verify</code> on the package directory with the Ed25519 public key you trust, before any host calls <code>register_package</code> or <code>registerPackage</code>. A bad signature, digest, or key exits non-zero. The host repeats those checks at registration and refuses the same package.</p>

  <h2>Install the CLI</h2>
  <p>The crate is <code>traverse-cli-rs</code>. The binary is <code>traverse-cli</code>. It needs Rust 1.95 or newer. The crate named <code>traverse-cli</code> on crates.io is a different tool.</p>
  <pre><code>{installSnippet}</code></pre>
  <p>That line is the <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/v0.17.1/README.md">v0.17.1 README</a>. The packaging guide at the same tag still shows <code>--version =0.16.0</code> in its install block.</p>

  <h2>Verify the package</h2>
  <p><code>out/digits</code> is the directory that holds <code>model.manifest.json</code>, <code>model.wasm</code>, and <code>model.sig.json</code>. <code>model.sig.json</code> is a detached Ed25519 signature over the exact manifest bytes.</p>
  <pre><code>{verifySnippet}</code></pre>
  <p>The command checks the signature, the trusted key, the manifest and rights (including <code>rights.derivation</code>), the WASM digest, the target, the host ceilings you pass, and that the guest imports nothing. Success prints <code>ok</code>, the identity, the digest, <code>imports</code>, and the verified <code>rights_record</code>.</p>
  <p>Pass <code>--model-usage commercial</code> or <code>--model-usage non_commercial</code> so the check matches the app. Without the flag, <code>verification_usage</code> stays <code>non_commercial</code> and <code>effective_usage</code> is null, which is why a <code>prohibited</code> package can still verify. <code>--require-model-usage</code> with no <code>--model-usage</code> reproduces <code>usage_undeclared</code>. A value such as <code>research</code> fails. With <code>--json</code>, flag and usage errors print a JSON object on stdout with <code>error.code</code> <code>usage_error</code> and exit 5.</p>

  <h2>Failures worth knowing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>signature_invalid</code>: the manifest bytes changed after signing. Sign again.</li>
    <li><code>key_untrusted</code>: the public key is not one you passed. Hosts must trust that same key.</li>
    <li><code>digest_mismatch</code>: <code>model.wasm</code> does not match <code>wasm_digest</code>. Update the manifest, then sign again.</li>
    <li><code>rights_incomplete</code> or <code>rights_inconsistent</code>: a rights field is empty, or <code>commercial_use</code> is more permissive than the derivation source.</li>
    <li><code>manifest_invalid</code>: unknown fields, an unsupported schema or ABI, or a guest that imports anything.</li>
    <li><code>host_limit_exceeded</code>: declared limits are above the ceilings you passed with <code>--max-package-bytes</code>, <code>--max-memory-bytes</code>, or <code>--max-fuel</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the host does next</h2>
  <p>Registration verifies the signature, the trusted key, that the manifest digest equals exactly one pin, the schema, the rights, the target, and the WASM digest. Each execute re-hashes the cached bytes against the pin. There is no download during execute. Trust lists live on the host (<code>TrustedModelKeys</code> on Rust, <code>trustedPublicKeysHex</code> on web and Swift). An app manifest cannot add a key.</p>

  <h2>Production keys in v0.17.1</h2>
  <p>At tag v0.17.1, <code>TRAVERSE_MODEL_SIGNING_KEYS</code> is an empty list in <code>traverse-runtime</code> and in <code>traverse-embedder-web</code>. <code>keys/model-signing/</code> contains the README and no <code>.pub</code> file. Hosts trust only keys you pass. There is no default trust and no network lookup. <code>digits-mlp-1.0.0</code> in that tag is signed with the test-only key. Keep that key out of a production trust list.</p>
  <p>The production procedure is written down: one dedicated Ed25519 key, separate from the registry capability key, held as <code>MODEL_SIGNING_KEY_HEX</code> in the <code>model-signing</code> GitHub Environment, with public keys committed under <code>keys/model-signing/</code> and mirrored into <code>TRAVERSE_MODEL_SIGNING_KEYS</code>. That is <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/v0.17.1/docs/adr/0080-model-signing-key-management.md">ADR-0080</a> and the <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/v0.17.1/docs/model-signing-key-runbook.md">model-signing key runbook</a>. The v0.17.1 tree does not contain that public key. Status of the program: <a href="/questions/is-production-model-signing-ready.html">Is production model signing ready?</a></p>
  <p>A registry capability uses a sibling <code>signature.json</code>. That check is <a href="/questions/how-do-i-verify-a-signed-capability-artifact.html">How do I verify a signed capability artifact?</a> The model command reference is <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/v0.17.1/docs/cli-reference.md">docs/cli-reference.md</a> and the worked example is <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/v0.17.1/docs/model-packaging-guide.md">docs/model-packaging-guide.md</a>, both at tag v0.17.1.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
